  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1259 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nDry & hot this weekend. A tropical system in the Gulf currently \nTropical Depression Nine is expected to intensify and make \nlandfall tracking up the Mississippi Delta. The only impact across\nour forecast area will be increasing pressure heights/ridging \nbuilding west of this post-tropical systems upper low which could \nmake Sunday & Monday slightly hotter before a potential cooldown \nstarting on Tuesday. Models in fairly good agreement with a trough\nforming in the main jet stream over the U.S. Pacific Northwest \nthen digging over the eastern Pacific Ocean off the coast of \nCalifornia further interacting with a weakening tropical system \n(currently Hurricane Rachel). This large amplitude trough will \nproduce an initial split-flow in the main jet stream as it comes \nashore early next week advancing eastward across the Southwestern \nU.S. toward the Southern Plains. This approaching Pacific system \npushing a cold front will bring our next return of rain/storms \nchances and cooler temperatures. Rain/storm chances will be low \n(20%) on Tuesday but increasing Tuesday night into mid-week when \na stronger cooldown may be felt. This far out in time the models \nare inconsistent with the timing of the cold front with the ECMWF \n& NBM solutions pushing it through Tuesday verses the GFS solution\n48-hours later.",
